> > 
> > Since I am now training for a career as a designer/engineer of social
> > institutions, I strongly agree with Tim.  I'm not ashamed to call
> > myself a "social engineer", and see nothing wrong with using math,
> > even physics-like, in the process.
> 
>   The problem I see with most social engineering is that it ultimately
> relies on the human agent, and since any model trivializes the
> complexity of that agent, there is bound to be a lag in the system.
> (and if that system is imposed by law, the human ends up being
> hurt when his personality and lifestyle doesn't fit the model)
> There are useful concepts to be learned, but I find it abhorrent
> to try to impose an engineered system on people (which is the way
> many social engineers *test* their system) Macroeconomics being
> a good example of a bad science wihch is used in bad ways and often
> ends up hurting a lot of people.
> 
>    I'm not accusing Robin of wanting to do any of this, but let's
> remember where many "social engineers" end up -- government.
> 
> 
Sorry to repeat all this....but the thread is diverse.
True social engineering success would seem to be based on natural
tendencies. Any engineer/architect knows that the best designs go with the
flow of the existing substructure. To design an edifice, every parameter
and property of every part of the structure is known to the nth degree.
How can you hope to put together a social structure that works when there
are so many unknowns?
Communism was put down in a previous post (or two...;-)...but it works
wonderfully for ants. All quantities are known..._all_ work for the good
of the whole...
It's a beautiful system, but it lacks any imagination...;-) But there are
few as dreaded social groups as a commune of ants on the march....
The point of all this...?
That if you work out a social system, it will only work if all the variables
are known... if the system is static (ant-like) it works as long as the system
exists. If the variables are constantly varying (:-)...the system may
work for a while, but falls apart due to chaos theory sooner or later...
Unless of course the system is somehow a dynamic one that changes and
takes into account all the variances.....but then it's a different system
now, isn't it?


