I agree.  Particularly, designing a system that is not tolerant of
failure in it's parts, or has no dynamic stability, has problems.

There are several types of social structures (in the right
local settings) that have proven to be very stable.  Tribal structures
in very-low population settings with little or no outside communication
have in some cases (Australian aboriginals) been stable for periods
exceeding 10,000 years, as far as evidence seems to present.

> Communism was put down in a previous post (or two...;-)...but it works
> wonderfully for ants. All quantities are known..._all_ work for the good
> of the whole...
> It's a beautiful system, but it lacks any imagination...;-) But there are
> few as dreaded social groups as a commune of ants on the march....

Ants have a system that is, on the whole, dynamically stable.  However,
I wouldn't say *all* quantities are known.  There is definitely a chemical
mediation system that can be disrupted, for example.  It is disrupted
in the case of certain beetles that take advantage of the chemical
identifier to get nurse-ants to feed them like the larvae, for example.
Etc.

Well, my point is that there is no ability to not cooperate in that
kind of example, for better or worse.

> The point of all this...?
> That if you work out a social system, it will only work if all the variables
> are known... if the system is static (ant-like) it works as long as the
> system exists.

When you say a "social system", on what scale are you referring?  Ants
compete on the level of hives.  Humans, by and large, seem to naturally
congregate in smaller groups , "families", or "tribes".  It seems to
me that the question is not one of an understood system, but
one of figuring out the details of why people congregate and/or
compete and what kinds of tactics, groupings, etc. they tend
to naturally use, trained or not.  If you figure that out, then
you probably have a good crack at trying to understand larger social
systems.

A "coffee-table"-level book on this that can perk one's interest, at
least, is "The Evolution of Cooperation" (can't remember the author(s)).
There are better treatises, but I can't remember any titles offhand.
Basically, it uses some points of game theory, for example, the well-
known "Prisoner's Dilemma".  Many assumptions are made and not well-
explained to the reader, but it get's people's attention somewhat.

> If the variables are constantly varying (:-)...the system may
> work for a while, but falls apart due to chaos theory sooner or later...
> Unless of course the system is somehow a dynamic one that changes and
> takes into account all the variances.....but then it's a different system
> now, isn't it?

I don't quite get the point of this part.  Just because something
is "chaotic" does not imply that it can't be stable, nor does a dynamically
stable system necessarily have to be understood in terms of static
"snapshots" of its states.

