Subject: [#94-5-36] filtering

One funny feature of the filter algo I suggested is this:  
Suppose Eric likes Tim and Tim dislikes Eric.  (Names are 
generic; any resemblance to CURRENT members of the List is 
purely coincidental.)  Eric would then tend to chase Tim 
through n-space, just like the two ramships in "The Ethics 
of Madness". 

Ray anticipated, in part, what I was about to say next:

When you rate a message, a more sophisticated filterer compares 
your rating to those of all others who have previously rated the 
same message; you and each other rater attract or repel each other 
according to the product of your ratings.

Then the filter predicts your rating of a new message as the sum, over all others who have already rated it, of :
	dotprod (vector[you], vector[Eric]) * rating [Eric,msg].

If no one has rated it yet, I suppose the rating used is
	dotprod (vector[you], vector[author]),
or the system can leave you to train a neural net on 
what you like.  ;)

Should the vector-keeper guard against dimensional collapse, 
i.e. convergence to a subspace of dimension lower than that of 
the underlying space?  On one hand, such collapse is irreversible 
unless noise is injected or a new user is added away from the 
hyperplane; on the other hand, collapse is valuable information 
that the number of dimensions can be reduced in the next version.

